{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:PredictedSpectra.Cl2PentagonAndFaddeevPartial","@type":"LeanModule","name":"PredictedSpectra.Cl2PentagonAndFaddeevPartial","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.ClausenFunctionMathlibFiller","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.ThreeSubstantiveMathlibFillers","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.partial_closures_at_boundary","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.partial_closures_at_boundary","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/predicted-spectra/cl2-pentagon-and-faddeev-partial.lean#L116-L131","doc":"**Joint partial-closure theorem (sorry-free).** With the\nboundary cases for both Cl_2 pentagon and Faddeev-Kashaev\nlimit closed, the §3b-cond conditional theorems are\nconsistent at the boundary.\n\nThis does *not* close the full unconditional content of\neither gap — that requires Mathlib upstream contributions\n(Fourier-series infrastructure for Cl_2 functional equations,\nuniform Tauberian for Faddeev-Kashaev). It does formally\ndischarge the boundary-consistency requirements that any\nfull closure must satisfy. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:cl2-pentagon-and-faddeev-partial","label":"prop:cl2-pentagon-and-faddeev-partial","md":"quantum-observable-universe/predicted-spectra/cl2-pentagon-and-faddeev-partial.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.faddeev_kashaev_at_zero_exact","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.faddeev_kashaev_at_zero_exact","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/predicted-spectra/cl2-pentagon-and-faddeev-partial.lean#L102-L112","doc":"**Faddeev-Kashaev finiteness condition.** For `x = 0` the\nrescaled log of the partial product is identically zero —\na stronger statement than convergence: it equals the limit\nexactly for every `(q, n)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.faddeev_kashaev_consistency_at_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.faddeev_kashaev_consistency_at_zero","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/predicted-spectra/cl2-pentagon-and-faddeev-partial.lean#L82-L100","doc":"**Faddeev-Kashaev boundary-consistency theorem.** With the\n`FaddeevKashaevQuasiClassicalLimit 0` typeclass in scope\n(already inhabited unconditionally via `archimedeanLimit_zero`),\nthe asserted convergence `(1 - q)·log Φ_n(0; q) → Li_2(0)`\nis realised *exactly* — not merely in the limit — at the\nboundary input `x = 0`: for every `(q, n)` the rescaled log of\nthe partial product already equals `Li_2(0)`.\n\nThis is the genuine boundary content the trivial `convergence_holds`\nfield stands in for: the class hypothesis is consumed (its presence\nis required to state the consistency of the named surrogate with the\nunconditional boundary identity `faddeev_limit_at_zero`), and the\nconclusion is the real equation, derived — not `True`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.faddeev_kashaev_at_zero_unconditional","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.faddeev_kashaev_at_zero_unconditional","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/predicted-spectra/cl2-pentagon-and-faddeev-partial.lean#L71-L80","doc":"**Faddeev-Kashaev at `x = 0`** (already in\n`dilogarithm-archimedean-limit.lean` as the\n`archimedeanLimit_zero` instance). We re-export it as a\ntheorem in this file's namespace for downstream\nconvenience. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.Cl_2_pentagon_consistent_with_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.Cl_2_pentagon_consistent_with_zero","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/predicted-spectra/cl2-pentagon-and-faddeev-partial.lean#L58-L65","doc":"**Pentagon identity is consistent with `Cl_2(0) = 0` at the\nboundary.** Trivial but important: the pentagon-holds class\nmust agree with the unconditional boundary lemma. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.Cl_2_pentagon_zero_substitution","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.Cl_2_pentagon_zero_substitution","kind":"theorem","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/predicted-spectra/cl2-pentagon-and-faddeev-partial.lean#L49-L56","doc":"**Pentagon identity with all-zero substitution.** Even without\nthe `Cl_2_PentagonHolds` typeclass, the all-zero case is\nunconditional. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.Cl_2_pentagon_at_zero_unconditional","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.PartialClosures.Cl_2_pentagon_at_zero_unconditional","kind":"theorem","line":39,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/predicted-spectra/cl2-pentagon-and-faddeev-partial.lean#L39-L47","doc":"**Pentagon identity at zero arguments.** When all five\narguments of the 5-term identity are zero, the identity\nholds unconditionally (each Cl_2(0) = 0 by `Cl_2_zero`,\nThe alternating sum is `0 - 0 + 0 - 0 + 0 = 0`). ","signatureChunk":0}]}